Convert a number with the input base in effect when it is pushed.
A numeric constant was pushed as a reference to its digits and only
converted the first time an operation used it, with whatever ibase
was current then. In dc, which runs each command as it is parsed, a
change of input base therefore changed numbers pushed before it:
"FF 16 i p" printed 255 rather than 99, and "11 2 i p" printed 3
rather than 11. In bc the same happened to an operand already pushed
when an assignment to ibase later in the expression ran. Convert the
constant when it is pushed, as GNU bc and dc do.

---
 miscutils/bc.c |   11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/miscutils/bc.c
+++ b/miscutils/bc.c
@@ -6789,12 +6789,19 @@
 			dbg_exec("BC_INST_builtin:");
 			s = zxc_program_builtin(inst);
 			break;
-		case XC_INST_NUM:
+		case XC_INST_NUM: {
+			BcNum *num;
 			dbg_exec("XC_INST_NUM:");
 			r.t = XC_RESULT_CONSTANT;
 			r.d.id.idx = xc_program_index(code, &ip->inst_idx);
-			bc_vec_push(&G.prog.results, &r);
+			// Convert it now, with the current ibase: a later change
+			// of ibase must not change a number already pushed
+			// (dc: "FF 16 i p")
+			s = zxc_program_num(&r, &num);
+			if (!s)
+				bc_vec_push(&G.prog.results, &r);
 			break;
+		}
 		case XC_INST_POP:
 			dbg_exec("XC_INST_POP:");
 			if (!STACK_HAS_MORE_THAN(&G.prog.results, 0))
